---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro'
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro'
import EnvTable from '../components/EnvTable.astro'
import Step from '../components/Step.astro'
import { parseEnvExample, readRepoFile } from '../lib/repo'

const entries = parseEnvExample(readRepoFile('docker/.env.example'))
const cloudflare = readRepoFile('docker/compose.cloudflare.yaml')
---
<Page
  title="Self-host PSPad"
  description="Run PSPad on your own server: a quick start with Docker Compose, hosted or bundled MongoDB and Keycloak, a reverse proxy or a Cloudflare Tunnel, and how I host it myself."
>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Self-host</h1>
    <p>Your own server, one Compose file, about ten minutes.</p>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <a href="#need">What you need</a>
      <a href="#quick-start">Quick start</a>
      <a href="#options">Options</a>
      <a href="#how-i-host-it">How I host it</a>
      <a href="#maintenance">Maintenance</a>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="need">What you need</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A Linux machine with Docker Compose v2 and about 2 GB of disk.</li>
      <li>A domain, if anyone but you will reach it.</li>
      <li>MongoDB and Keycloak are bundled. Both can be yours instead, see <a href="#options">Options</a>.</li>
      <li>Optional: outbound HTTPS from the API to <code>api.nbp.pl</code>. Only a budget's exchange-rate lookups use it; without it the NBP button fails and everything else works.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
    <p>Everything on <code>localhost</code>, for a first look.</p>

    <Step n={1} title="Clone">
      <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/psp515/PSPad.git
cd PSPad</code></pre>
    </Step>

    <Step n={2} title="Create the environment file">
      <pre><code>cp docker/.env.example docker/.env</code></pre>
      <p>The defaults work on <code>localhost</code>. Change the two passwords before the machine is reachable from anywhere else. Every value, for later:</p>
      <details>
        <summary>All variables</summary>
        <EnvTable entries={entries} />
      </details>
    </Step>

    <Step n={3} title="Start">
      <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
      <p>The first build takes a few minutes. When this prints <code>401</code> the API is up and you are simply not signed in yet:</p>
      <pre><code>curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{'{'}http_code{'}'}\n" http://localhost:5000/api/today</code></pre>
    </Step>

    <Step n={4} title="Create your first user">
      <p>Accounts live in Keycloak. Open <code>http://localhost:8080</code>, sign in as <code>admin</code> / <code>admin</code>, switch the realm from <strong>master</strong> to <strong>psplace</strong>, then <strong>Users → Create new user</strong>. Under <strong>Credentials</strong> set a password with <em>Temporary</em> off.</p>
    </Step>

    <Step n={5} title="Sign in">
      <p>Open <code>http://localhost:5001</code> and sign in. My Day is empty at first while three starter areas are created. Install it from the browser's address bar for the offline app.</p>
      <Callout kind="warning" title="Give it 20–30 seconds before you trust something is saved">
        <p>New tasks save to your browser at once and reach the server within 20–30 seconds. Restart the stack sooner and they are gone.</p>
      </Callout>
    </Step>

    <h2 id="options">Options</h2>
    <p>Each option is one line in <code>docker/.env</code>. <code>COMPOSE_PROFILES</code> says which bundled services start; remove a name to bring your own.</p>

    <h3 id="mongodb">MongoDB: bundled or hosted</h3>
    <p>Hosted means any MongoDB 8 replica set the server can reach. The free Atlas M0 tier is enough for one household:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Create a cluster at <a href="https://www.mongodb.com/atlas">Atlas</a>, then a database user with <strong>Read and write to any database</strong>.</li>
      <li>Under <strong>Network Access</strong> allow your server's public address.</li>
      <li><strong>Connect → Drivers</strong>: copy the <code>mongodb+srv://</code> string and put the user's password in it.</li>
      <li>In <code>docker/.env</code>:
        <pre><code>COMPOSE_PROFILES=local-keycloak
MONGO_CONNECTION_STRING=mongodb+srv://pspad:PASSWORD@cluster0.abcde.mongodb.net/pspad?retryWrites=true&amp;w=majority</code></pre>
      </li>
      <li><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d</code>. The <code>mongo</code> container is not started.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Atlas keeps its own backups, so the <a href="#backups">dump command</a> below does not apply.</p>

    <h3 id="keycloak">Keycloak: bundled or your own</h3>
    <p>Your own Keycloak needs the realm from <code>docker/keycloak/realm-psplace.json</code> (<strong>Realm settings → Action → Partial import</strong>), or any realm with a public client <code>pspad-frontend</code> and an audience <code>pspad-backend</code>. Then in <code>docker/.env</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>COMPOSE_PROFILES=local-mongo
KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY=https://auth.example.com/realms/psplace
KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_AUTHORITY=https://auth.example.com/realms/psplace
KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME=https://auth.example.com
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=…
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=…</code></pre>
    <p>The admin credentials are what the API uses to remove a login when a user deletes their account. Rotate the password there and here together.</p>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Two Keycloak URLs">
      <p><code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> is how the <strong>browser</strong> reaches Keycloak, <code>KEYCLOAK_INTERNAL_AUTHORITY</code> how the <strong>API container</strong> does. With your own Keycloak they are the same address.</p>
    </Callout>

    <h3 id="internet">Reaching it from the internet</h3>
    <p>Either way, set the four public URLs in <code>docker/.env</code> first, then update the Keycloak client:</p>
    <pre><code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS=https://pspad.example.com
API_BASE_ADDRESS=https://pspad-api.example.com
KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY=https://auth.example.com/realms/psplace
KEYCLOAK_HOSTNAME=https://auth.example.com</code></pre>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Update the Keycloak client, or sign-in fails">
      <p>The bundled realm has <code>http://localhost:5001/*</code> as the redirect URI. In Keycloak: realm <strong>psplace → Clients → pspad-frontend → Valid redirect URIs</strong>, set it to your app URL followed by <code>/*</code>.</p>
    </Callout>
    <p><strong>A reverse proxy on the server</strong> (nginx, Caddy):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Three hostnames to the published ports: app <code>5001</code>, API <code>5000</code>, Keycloak <code>8080</code>. TLS at the proxy, HSTS on.</li>
      <li>Forward <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code>; nginx: <code>proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;</code>.</li>
      <li>Set <code>API_TRUSTED_PROXY_NETWORK</code> to the stack's Docker subnet so rate limits see each visitor: <code>docker network inspect pspad_pspad --format '{'{'}range .IPAM.Config{'}'}{'{'}.Subnet{'}'}{'{'}end{'}'}'</code>.</li>
      <li>Keep port <code>5000</code> off the internet: bind it to <code>127.0.0.1</code> or close it at the firewall.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>A Cloudflare Tunnel</strong>: no open ports at all. That is how I host it, next.</p>
    <Callout title="The browser only talks to the origins in .env">
      <p>The client's security policy and the API's allowed origins are built from <code>APP_BASE_ADDRESS</code>, <code>API_BASE_ADDRESS</code> and <code>KEYCLOAK_AUTHORITY</code> when the containers start. A "Refused to connect" error in the browser console means one of them is wrong: fix it and recreate the stack.</p>
    </Callout>

    <!-- how-i-host-it -->

    <h2 id="maintenance">Maintenance</h2>

    <h3 id="backups">Backups</h3>
    <p>Three volumes hold everything:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-data</code> — areas, lists, tasks, the event log.</li>
      <li><code>pspad_mongo-keyfile</code> — the replica-set key. Lose it and <code>mongo-data</code> won't start.</li>
      <li><code>pspad_keycloak-data</code> — accounts and realm config.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Never pass -v to docker compose down">
      <p>
        <code>down -v</code> deletes <code>pspad_keycloak-data</code>, and with it every
        account. PSPad keys its users by the Keycloak user's UUID, so accounts recreated
        afterwards are new people — the old areas, lists and tasks stay in MongoDB, owned by
        a user that can no longer sign in. <code>down</code> on its own is safe.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p>Dump the database:</p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml exec -T mongo \
  sh -c 'mongodump --archive --username pspad --password "$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD" --authenticationDatabase admin' \
  > pspad-backup.archive</code></pre>
    <p>
      Keep the single quotes — the password only exists inside the <code>mongo</code>
      container. Double quotes would expand it on your host instead, where it's empty.
    </p>

    <h3>Upgrading, restarting</h3>
    <pre><code>git pull
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml up -d --build</code></pre>
    <p>
      Volumes survive — only <code>down -v</code> destroys them. Give Mongo 10–30 seconds to
      report healthy again before assuming something's broken.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every image is pinned to an exact version, so an upgrade changes MongoDB, Keycloak
      or nginx only when <code>compose.yaml</code> or a <code>Dockerfile</code> in the
      release does.
    </p>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Upgrading from a release without profiles">
      <p>Older <code>docker/.env</code> files have no <code>COMPOSE_PROFILES</code> line, and without it the bundled MongoDB and Keycloak no longer start. Add <code>COMPOSE_PROFILES=local-mongo,local-keycloak</code> to <code>docker/.env</code> before <code>up</code>. Nothing else changes.</p>
    </Callout>
    <Callout kind="warning" title="Upgrading from a release with floating images">
      <p>
        Older releases pulled <code>mongo:8</code> and Keycloak 26.0. This release pins
        MongoDB 8.3.11 and Keycloak 26.7.4, which migrates its embedded database on first
        start. Before you run <code>up</code>, take a <a href="#backups">database dump</a> and
        export the <strong>psplace</strong> realm. Nothing else changes: same
        <code>.env</code>, same ports, same volumes, and none of them needs new file
        permissions.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3>Live updates through a proxy</h3>
    <p>
      An open app holds one long-lived request, <code>GET /api/live</code>, a Server-Sent
      Events stream that tells it to sync the moment something changes. The headers and the 25-second heartbeat are chosen so stock proxy
      settings carry the stream.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cloudflare (proxied DNS or Tunnel): expected to need no change; not tested
      here.</strong> Per its documented behaviour Cloudflare streams
      <code>text/event-stream</code> unbuffered and drops an origin connection silent for
      100 s (error 524); the 25 s heartbeat stays under that. Browsers talk HTTP/2
      to Cloudflare, so a stream does not use up the per-host connection limit. Only if a
      Cache Rule caches the API hostname, exclude <code>/api/live</code> (better: never cache
      the API).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>nginx in front of the API: stock settings were tested and need no change.</strong>
      Change only if the config has one of these:
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>If your config has</th><th>Change it to</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>proxy_read_timeout</code> below 30s</td><td>≥ 60s, at least for <code>location /api/live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>proxy_ignore_headers X-Accel-Buffering</code></td><td>remove it, or add <code>proxy_buffering off;</code> in <code>location /api/live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>proxy_cache</code> on the API</td><td><code>proxy_cache off;</code> for <code>/api/live</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>gzip_types</code> including <code>text/event-stream</code></td><td>remove it</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TLS without <code>http2</code> and no Cloudflare in front</td><td><code>http2 on;</code> — on HTTP/1.1 each visible tab holds one of the browser's six connections to the API (hidden tabs drop their stream); the default proxy-less <code>http://localhost:5000</code> is HTTP/1.1 too</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>An explicit block that does not rely on the response headers:</p>
    <pre><code>{`location /api/live {
    proxy_pass http://<api-upstream>;
    proxy_buffering off;
    proxy_cache off;
    proxy_read_timeout 1h;
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Connection "";
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      <strong>Caddy: expected to need no change; not tested here.</strong> Per its documented
      behaviour it flushes <code>text/event-stream</code> immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
      An integration test runs the stream through
      <code>nginxinc/nginx-unprivileged:1.31.6-alpine</code> with a stock
      <code>proxy_pass</code> block and a short <code>proxy_read_timeout</code>. Cloudflare and
      Caddy are not tested.
    </p>

    <details>
      <summary>Give the API its own database user</summary>
    <p>
      Out of the box the API signs in to MongoDB as the root user <code>pspad</code>. To
      limit what a leaked connection string can do, create a user that can read and write
      <code>MONGO_DATABASE</code> and nothing else, using the example script
      <code>docker/mongo-app-user.js</code>. Run it as root inside the running
      <code>mongo</code> container (use your own database name and a password of letters,
      digits and <code>- . _ ~</code>):
    </p>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml cp docker/mongo-app-user.js mongo:/tmp/mongo-app-user.js
docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml exec \
  -e MONGO_DATABASE=pspad -e MONGO_APP_USER=pspad-api -e MONGO_APP_PASSWORD='&lt;app password&gt;' \
  mongo sh -c 'mongosh --quiet -u pspad -p "$MONGO_INITDB_ROOT_PASSWORD" --authenticationDatabase admin /tmp/mongo-app-user.js'</code></pre>
    <p>
      It prints <code>created pspad-api on pspad</code>; running it again resets the password
      and roles. Then point the API at the new user with an override file next to
      <code>compose.yaml</code>, say <code>docker/compose.db-user.yaml</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>services:
  api:
    environment:
      Mongo__ConnectionString: mongodb://pspad-api:&lt;app password&gt;@mongo:27017/pspad?directConnection=true</code></pre>
    <pre><code>docker compose -f docker/compose.yaml -f docker/compose.db-user.yaml up -d</code></pre>
    <p>
      Pass both files on every later <code>up</code>, or the API goes back to the root user.
      The root user stays for the healthcheck, the script and your backups. Running PSPad
      against your own MongoDB or Atlas? Give its user only <code>readWrite</code> on the
      PSPad database.
    </p>
    </details>

    <Callout kind="warning" title="Two things worth knowing">
      <p>The bundled Keycloak runs in development mode, fine for one household behind a proxy, not for a public service. The old MongoDB keyfile is in the git history; the stack generates a fresh one on first start, so nothing in the repository unlocks your data.</p>
    </Callout>
  </div>
</Page>

<style>
  .toc { display: flex; gap: 16px; flex-wrap: wrap; margin: 16px 0 32px; font-weight: 500; }
  details { margin: 16px 0; }
  summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 500; }
</style>
